      "text": "1. We are given that $\\mathbf{u}$, $\\mathbf{v}$, and $\\mathbf{w}$ are unit vectors.\n2. We also know that $\\mathbf{w}$ can be expressed as $\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$\n3. We can substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{w}$ into the equation $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$\n4. This gives us: \\begin{align*}\n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u})\\times \\mathbf{u} &= \\mathbf{v} \\\\\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}) + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} &= \\mathbf{v} \\\\\n\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{0} &= \\mathbf{v}.\n\\end{align*}\n5. Thus, $\\mathbf{u},$ $\\mathbf{v},$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ must be linearly independent and their zeros must cancel out.\n6. A unit vector being orthogonal to itself implies it must be a scalar multiple of the $ \\textbf{i} $ and $ \\textbf{j} $ axes. We want to determine which one while attending it.\n7. If $\\mathbf{u}= (1,0,0),$ then $\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}= (0,1,0),$ and we should find out their dot products.\n8. Hence, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w})= \\mathbf{u}\\cdot((0,1,0)\\times(0,1,0))= \\mathbf{u}.\\cdot(0,0,0)=0.$\nThe answer is: 0",
