      "text": "We are given that $\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}$ and that $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Therefore, we have\n\\[\\mathbf{v} \\times (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.\\]By properties of the cross product, $\\mathbf{v} \\times (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}) = (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}.$\n\nBy the third rule of vector triple product, $\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u} = -\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v},$ so\n\\[(\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{v}.\\]Again, by the third rule, $(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u}.$  Since $\\mathbf{u}$\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ are unit vectors, and $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector that is also a unit vector, we must have\n\\[\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\boxed{0}.\\]The answer is: 0",
